@node	0.9, Chapter 1, 0.8, Chapter 0
@section Modules, crates, and reading this book

@subheading The workspace

This edition lives under @code{rust/}, a Cargo @newterm{workspace}: one
@code{Cargo.toml} at the root lists member @newterm{crates}, one crate per
book chapter (@code{crates/ch01} through @code{crates/ch05}), plus
@code{crates/sicp-runtime} for the types every chapter shares (@code{Value},
@code{Env}, @code{Stream}, and the rest) and @code{crates/ch00} for this
chapter. Each chapter crate follows the same shape:

@example text
crates/chNN/
  src/lib.rs          declares one module per section
  src/sec_X_Y.rs       that section's library code
  examples/*.rs        one file per book listing, with assertions
@end example

@noindent
and two top-level directories hold every exercise, outside the crates:
@code{exercises/chNN/sec_X_Y.rs} for pending scaffolds, and
@code{solutions/chNN/sec_X_Y.rs} for reference solutions, each wired into its
crate's @code{Cargo.toml} as its own test target. A listing from this
chapter, say the one in @ref{0.1}, runs with:

@example text
cargo run --example sec_0_1_bindings
@end example

@noindent
from inside @code{rust/}, and its assertions are also checked by
@code{cargo nextest run --workspace}, which runs every crate's tests
(including every example's own @code{#[test]}s where it has them) in one
pass. @code{cargo fmt --check} and @code{cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D
warnings} are the formatting and lint gates every commit in this edition
passes; running code is never required to read the book, only to check it or
extend it.

Every public function, type, and module in @code{crates/} carries a
@code{///} @newterm{doc comment} directly above it; a module's own summary is
a @code{//!} comment at the top of its file. These are not decoration:
@code{#![deny(missing_docs)]} is a workspace-wide lint, so an undocumented
public item is a compile error, and @code{cargo doc --open} turns every one
of these comments into the browsable API reference for this edition's
crates.

@subheading Reading a borrow-checker error

A rejected program in this book almost always fails with a specific,
readable diagnostic rather than a bare ``no.'' A typical one for
@ref{0.3}'s case A reads, in outline:

@example text
error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: `a`
 --> src/main.rs:3:23
  |
2 |     let b = a;
  |             - value moved here
3 |     println!("@{a@} @{b@}");
  |               ^^ value borrowed here after move
@end example

@noindent
The error code (@code{E0382} here), the line the value moved on, and the line
that tried to use it afterward are all present in every such message;
@code{rustc --explain E0382} prints a longer explanation of that error class
on demand. Learning to read one of these end to end, rather than pattern-
matching on the shape of the error, pays off for the rest of the book.

@subheading The interaction convention

Every runnable listing in this book, starting with the first one in
@ref{0.1}, is shown as one @code{@@example rust} block. A line of output is
followed by a comment giving the value or text that line produces; a result
comment always carries the marker @code{// =>}, as in

@example rust
println!("@{@}", 6 * 7);
// => 42
@end example

@noindent
while a line that only produces a side effect with no return value worth
naming (a loop's intermediate prints, for instance) is followed by a plain
@code{//} comment with no marker. The marker exists because the source this
book adapts prints its interpreter's responses in italics after each
expression; a comment is this edition's equivalent, and it stays a valid
Rust comment in every renderer, including a PDF with no syntax highlighting
at all. The value shown after @code{=>} is not asserted by the prose: it is
asserted by a real @code{assert_eq!} in the runnable file the listing names,
which fires when the reader runs the example with
@code{cargo run --example}. The verification gate compiles every example, so
a listing that stops compiling fails the gate; whether a printed value
agrees with the code is checked by running the example, not by the gate.

@subheading Exercise numbers and the exercise map

Every exercise in chapters 1 through 5 keeps its original number from the
source book; @code{docs/exercise-map.md} at the repository root records,
for every one of those 356 exercises, which of three things this edition did
with it: @code{T} (translate: only the syntax changed), @code{A} (adapt: the
idea survives, the statement changed, with the reason recorded), or @code{R}
(replace: the exercise was about Scheme itself, so a language-tailored
exercise under the same number replaces it). A replaced exercise's statement
opens with a fixed sentence naming the substitution:

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise @math{N.M} is about Scheme itself, so this edition
replaces it; the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same
section material.}
@end quotation

@noindent
and the rare added exercise, at most one per section, is numbered with a
lowercase letter after the exercise it extends (@code{2.73a}, for example)
and opens the same way:

@quotation
@emph{Exercise @math{N.Ma} is added by this edition and extends exercise
@math{N.M}; SICP numbers stop at @math{N.M}.}
@end quotation

This chapter is the one exception to all of it. Chapter 0 does not exist in
the source book, so its five exercises, numbered @code{0.1} through
@code{0.5} in the order they appear across this chapter's sections, are new
material this edition adds outside that numbering entirely; none of them
carries a signpost, because none of them replaces or extends anything. Every
exercise you have seen so far in this chapter, and every one in the chapters
ahead, has exactly three files: its scaffold, a failing test until solved; a
reference solution, a passing test once solved; and a short rationale beside
the solution explaining the approach. Running @code{cargo nextest run
--workspace --run-ignored ignored-only --no-tests=pass} lists exactly the
exercises still pending, by name, which is the standing way to see what is
left to solve without reading every file in @code{exercises/} by hand.

You now have the whole vocabulary chapter 1 assumes. It starts, as the source
book does, with values, procedures, and the substitution model, in Rust
syntax instead of Scheme's.
